  "query_preview": "/TONIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\n(Tonight - Thursday)\n\nIncreasing mid/high level clouds are expected by this evening as\na midlevel shortwave trough moves into the Upper Midwest. At the\nsurface, a cold front is expected to gradually progress southeastward\ninto northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois after 0600 UTC. A\nfew showers, and possibly a rumble or two of thunder, are expected\novernight in these locations. Due to the aforementioned increase in\ncloud cover, lows tonight will be on the milder side, generally in\nthe mid 60s to low 70s.\n\nChances of showers and thunderstorms increases by midday on Wednesday\nas another midlevel shortwave trough moves across the central Plains.\nThis trough is expected to be further south than its predecessor, with\nthe strongest mid/upper level forcing for ascent focused across\nnortheast Missouri and west-central Illinois. This is also where the\nsurface boundary should be located at this time, so widespread showers\nand thunderstorms are expected. The exact timing of this wave varies\na few hours amongst deterministic guidance, but has sped up compared\nto what it looked like yesterday. That means there should be quite a\nbit of showers and storms earlier in the day, which also means a bit\nless instability to work with. The latest HREF joint probabilities for \nat least 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE and 30+ knots of deep-layer shear do peak\nin the 50-70% range from central Missouri northeastward through\nwest-central Illinois however. Multicell clusters are likely to be the\npreferred convective mode, though a few transient supercell structures\nare also possible. Damaging winds are the main threat, though some\nguidance suggests a weak surface low transversing the surface\nbaroclinic zone. There may be enough low-level shear/helicity near the\ntrack of that low to yield a low threat for a brief, weak tornado.\n\nInstability weakens after sunset, with the severe threat likely ending\nshortly thereafter. A west/southwest 20-25+ knot low-level jet is\nexpected to develop overnight Wednesday night, bringing increasing\nmoisture convergence to parts of the area. The coverage of showers and\nthunderstorms should increase again as a result overnight, first near\nthe I-70 corridor before activity gradually sinks southward as the\nlow-level jet veers more to the west/northwest. There is a concern for\nsome training, with the jet oriented nearly parallel to the surface\nboundary in an environment favorable for efficient warm rainfall\nprocesses (deep warm cloud depths, precipitable water values > 90th\npercentile of climatology). Locally heavy rainfall is the main concern\nlate Wednesday night into very early Thursday morning.\n\nThere likely will be a lull in activity late Thursday morning into\nthe afternoon as the low-level jet weakens. The location of the\nsurface front may be a focused for additional development by the\nafternoon as diurnal instability increases. Another midlevel shortwave\ntrough is forecast to move out of the mid-Missouri Valley Thursday\nnight. This feature combined with a strengthening low-level jet is\nexpected to yield another uptick in shower and thunderstorm activity\nlate Thursday night. The best chances (40-60%) are near and just north\nof the expected location of the surface boundary, which is expected\nto be across southeast Missouri and southwest Illinois. Guidance suggests\nthat the front may wash out a bit by Friday afternoon, but wherever\nit is may have enough convergence to produce additional showers and\nthunderstorms.\n\n(Friday Night - Monday)\n\nEnsemble guidance is in a bit better agreement heading into the\nweekend, showing an amplifying mid/upper level ridge across the\nRockies/Plains. We will be on the eastern edge of the higher\nheights, but it does look like we are likely to head into at least\na short period of hotter and more humid conditions again starting\nSunday. Highs in the low 90s with increasing humidity will likely\nmean 100+ heat index values for much of the area both Sunday and\nMonday.",
